      q: "An economy produces only agricultural goods and manufactured goods, and its production possibility curve is drawn bowed outwards from the origin. (a) State what a point INSIDE the curve indicates and give one cause. (b) Explain why the curve is bowed outwards rather than a straight line. (c) Explain the difference between moving from a point inside the curve onto the curve, and the whole curve shifting outwards.",
      model: "(a) A point inside the curve shows that the economy is producing less than it could — resources are unemployed or being used inefficiently, so there is spare capacity. A cause would be a recession creating cyclical unemployment, or capital equipment lying idle.\n\n(b) The bowed-outwards (concave) shape shows INCREASING opportunity cost. Factors of production are not equally suited to both uses: the first resources transferred out of agriculture are the ones least suited to farming, so little agricultural output is lost for a large gain in manufactures. As transfer continues, increasingly good farmland and farm-specific skills have to be given up, so each extra unit of manufactured output costs more and more agricultural output. A straight-line PPC would instead mean opportunity cost was constant — that resources were perfectly substitutable between the two uses.\n\n(c) Moving from inside the curve ONTO it is a recovery: existing idle resources are put back to work, so actual output rises but the economy's maximum potential is unchanged. A SHIFT of the whole curve outwards is economic growth in the sense of rising potential: more or better factors of production — net investment in capital, a larger or better-trained workforce, new technology, newly discovered resources. The first is a change in how fully potential is used; the second is a change in the potential itself.",
