      model: "(a) The gas deposit is LAND — the factor covering all natural resources. Its reward is rent.\n\n(b) Output cannot rise until labour and capital are combined with the gas, and both are immobile in the short run. Labour is occupationally immobile: a worker trained in farming or retail cannot switch to gas engineering without years of training, so the specific skills simply do not exist in the domestic workforce yet. Labour may also be geographically immobile — the deposit may be far from where people live, and housing costs, family ties and poor transport discourage moving. Capital is immobile too: drilling equipment is specific to this use and takes time to design, order and install.\n\n(c) Invest in technical education and vocational training in engineering. This raises the quality of the labour force, reduces occupational immobility, and so raises the economy's productive potential — shown as an outward shift of the production possibility curve. It is a supply-side policy, so the benefit arrives only after several years, and there is an opportunity cost: the government spending could have funded healthcare instead.",
